### What is the problem?

Given an unsorted array and a value K, return the Kth largest element by value (not the Kth distinct). For example, in `[3,2,1,5,6,4]` with K=2, the answer is 5.

At the National Bank of India, the risk desk needs the Kth largest transaction to set an alert threshold.

### How does the min-heap approach work?

Push each element. When the heap exceeds K, poll the smallest. After one pass, the root is the Kth largest.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class KthLargestHeap {
    public static int findKthLargest(int[] amounts, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
        for (int amount : amounts) {
            minHeap.offer(amount);
            if (minHeap.size() > k) {
                minHeap.poll();
            }
        }
        return minHeap.peek();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] transactions = {5000, 2000, 8000, 1000, 9000, 3000};
        System.out.println("2nd largest: " + findKthLargest(transactions, 2));
    }
}
```

```text
2nd largest: 8000
```

### How does QuickSelect work?

Reframe the problem: the Kth largest is the element at index `n - K` in ascending sorted order. Partition around a pivot, then recurse into the correct side.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.Random;

public class KthLargestQuickSelect {
    private static final Random rand = new Random();

    public static int findKthLargest(int[] amounts, int k) {
        int target = amounts.length - k;
        return quickSelect(amounts, 0, amounts.length - 1, target);
    }

    private static int quickSelect(int[] arr, int lo, int hi, int target) {
        if (lo == hi) return arr[lo];

        int pivotIdx = lo + rand.nextInt(hi - lo + 1);
        swap(arr, pivotIdx, hi);
        int pivot = arr[hi];

        int storeIdx = lo;
        for (int i = lo; i < hi; i++) {
            if (arr[i] < pivot) {
                swap(arr, i, storeIdx);
                storeIdx++;
            }
        }
        swap(arr, storeIdx, hi);

        if (storeIdx == target) return arr[storeIdx];
        else if (target < storeIdx) return quickSelect(arr, lo, storeIdx - 1, target);
        else return quickSelect(arr, storeIdx + 1, hi, target);
    }

    private static void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
        int tmp = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = tmp;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] transactions = {5000, 2000, 8000, 1000, 9000, 3000};
        System.out.println("2nd largest: " + findKthLargest(transactions, 2));
    }
}
```

```text
2nd largest: 8000
```

### How do the two approaches compare?

| Aspect | Min-Heap | QuickSelect |
|---|---|---|
| Time (average) | O(n log K) | O(n) |
| Time (worst) | O(n log K) | O(n^2) without random pivot |
| Space | O(K) | O(1) extra (in-place) |
| Mutates input | No | Yes |
| Streaming | Yes | No (needs full array) |
| Simplicity | Simple | More complex |

### Why randomize the pivot in QuickSelect?

Without randomization, a sorted or nearly-sorted array causes the worst partition every time: one side gets n-1 elements and the other gets 0. This degrades to O(n^2). Randomizing the pivot makes bad partitions statistically rare.

```java
// Without random: worst case O(n^2) on sorted input
int pivotIdx = hi; // always picks the last element

// With random: expected O(n)
int pivotIdx = lo + rand.nextInt(hi - lo + 1);
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using a max-heap instead of a min-heap. A max-heap would need to hold all n elements and then poll K-1 times, which is O(n log n) rather than O(n log K).

```java
// Wrong: max-heap needs all elements
PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
for (int x : arr) maxHeap.offer(x);
for (int i = 0; i < k - 1; i++) maxHeap.poll();
return maxHeap.peek(); // O(n log n)

// Correct: size-K min-heap
PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
for (int x : arr) {
    minHeap.offer(x);
    if (minHeap.size() > k) minHeap.poll();
}
return minHeap.peek(); // O(n log K)
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **K = 1**: return the maximum element.
- **K = n**: return the minimum element.
- **All elements equal**: any element is the Kth largest.
- **Array of size 1**: return the single element (K must be 1).

### What should a senior engineer know?

The trade-off between heap and QuickSelect is what interviewers want. State it clearly:

- Heap: simple, worst-case O(n log K), streaming-friendly, does not mutate input.
- QuickSelect: average linear, mutates input, needs full array, requires randomization.

For the Kth smallest, the same approaches apply: use a max-heap of size K, or target index K-1 in QuickSelect.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What if data arrives as a stream? (Use the min-heap, updated on each arrival.)
- What if K is very close to n? (The heap holds almost all elements; QuickSelect is better.)
- Can you guarantee O(n) worst case? (Use median-of-medians as the pivot selection, but it is rarely practical.)
